Code Guide for Random Spatial Split

configs/gru/gru.yaml:
lines 23-30 set the parameters for the spatial split (lines 26-28 get ignored if spatial split is random)

src/scripts/joint/temporal/gru_train.py:
line 141 creates the config loader
lines 166-171 reads the spatial split block from the config file
line 201 calls resolve_split_path (lines 19-39 in spatial_split.py, just creates a unique file name and path for the split)
line 202-213 calls load_or_create_split from spatial_split_py

—> src/scripts/libs/spatial_split.py:
lines 170-207: load_or_create_split, checks if the split file already exists and calls the function for the right split type if it doesn’t

lines 115-129: spatial_split random
- loads all unique well ids
- in line 118, the number n of training wells is computed
- in line 119, n random samples are taken for the train set
- in line 121, i initialize the dataframe to include a cluster column even though we don’t need it for this split, to match other parts of my architecture
- in line 122, wells in the newly created dataframe are assigned to either train or holdout set
- lines 124/125 create a path and save a csv for the new split
- lines 127/128 create a new dataset that only contains the train wells so the models don’t see holdout wells at all during training, and returns that alongside the full split file


after gru training: src/scripts/joint/temporal/gru_eval.py:
line 170: since the split now exists, it only reads it this time


after gru finishes —> interpolation with gp:
src/scripts/joint/spatial/gp_eval.py:
line 217: only calls resolve_split_path, uses the holdout well ids from that file to get the true observations of the holdout wells for performance evaluation
